## 4. Method (applied in the same order to every table)

| Step | What was done | Why |
|---|---|---|
| 1. Read as text | `dtype=str, keep_default_na=False` | Stops pandas guessing types or hiding placeholders |
| 2. Trim and null | Strip spaces; turn `NA`, `N/A`, `null`, `None`, `-`, `?` into real nulls | One kind of "missing" |
| 3. Standardise labels | `standardise()` = exact match, then alias dictionary, then fuzzy match (`difflib`, cutoff 0.7); an "unmatched" report lists anything not matched | Fixes spelling and format variants without losing values |
| 4. Parse dates | Try each known format in turn; failures become null; future dates (after 28 Oct 2026) become null | Avoids day/month confusion |
| 5. Convert numbers | Pull the number out of text; null negatives and values outside business limits | Impossible values are never guessed |
| 6. Rebuild derived fields | Length of stay, actual hours, overtime, day name, quarter, region, department, `Operational_Flag`, `Cleaning_Completed_Flag` | A calculated field must agree with its source |
| 7. Fix logic | Swap reversed start/end times; add one day to night-shift ends; align status with discharge and disposition | Internal consistency |
| 8. Remove duplicates | (a) exact copies, (b) same key: keep the most complete row, (c) same record under a new ID: use a business key | Each kind of duplicate needs its own rule |
| 9. Check links | Anti-join every foreign key against its parent table | Referential integrity |
| 10. Recover what can be recovered | Use related data (see section 6) | Fewer gaps without inventing values |
| 11. Label what is left | Text categories become `Unknown`; numbers and dates stay `NULL` | Honest handling of gaps |

